1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,284
Every order a company ships comes with a promise:

2
00:00:03,284 --> 00:00:04,161
a delivery date.

3
00:00:04,481 --> 00:00:07,831
Aisha Rahman in Liverpool was promised hers by December 14th.

4
00:00:08,481 --> 00:00:11,683
On October 20th, a cyber incident shuts the terminals at the

5
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:12,643
Port of Singapore.

6
00:00:12,963 --> 00:00:14,831
Her parcel never goes near Singapore.

7
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:15,942
It will still be late.

8
00:00:16,692 --> 00:00:18,644
Radio modules get stuck at the port.

9
00:00:18,644 --> 00:00:21,898
The circuit boards waiting on them slip, then the smart home

10
00:00:21,898 --> 00:00:24,447
devices, the warehouse restock, and her parcel.

11
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:26,971
Four steps down the chain, she's three days late.

12
00:00:27,291 --> 00:00:32,543
Across the network: 755 late orders, $114,000 in fees, and

13
00:00:32,543 --> 00:00:34,535
nobody sees it coming.

14
00:00:35,785 --> 00:00:36,954
This is Promise Graph.

15
00:00:37,274 --> 00:00:40,695
It finds every broken promise, works out what to rescue, and

16
00:00:40,695 --> 00:00:43,033
runs AI agents that remember what worked.

17
00:00:44,283 --> 00:00:46,127
Underneath is a FalkorDB graph:

18
00:00:46,127 --> 00:00:49,755
ports, lanes, shipments that feed each other, and orders with

19
00:00:49,755 --> 00:00:50,290
promises.

20
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:52,458
A closed port is a starting node.

21
00:00:52,458 --> 00:00:55,369
The damage is a path, and one Cypher query walks it.

22
00:00:57,819 --> 00:01:00,912
Ask in plain English, and you get one sentence and a graph of

23
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:01,977
only what answers it.

24
00:01:02,297 --> 00:01:04,465
The AI only picks a question template.

25
00:01:04,465 --> 00:01:06,234
Every number comes from Cypher.

26
00:01:08,285 --> 00:01:10,381
See it on the globe, or step by step:

27
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:13,779
what FalkorDB walked, how long it took, and the exact query.

28
00:01:16,529 --> 00:01:18,099
Ask why Aisha's order is late:

29
00:01:18,099 --> 00:01:21,343
the chain back to the port, then the same chain on a calendar.

30
00:01:24,793 --> 00:01:28,092
Or ask for the costliest orders, what to rescue first, or a way

31
00:01:28,092 --> 00:01:28,930
around the port.

32
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:35,176
Then six AI agents take over, and they coordinate through the

33
00:01:35,176 --> 00:01:35,490
graph.

34
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:39,601
Sentinel recalls similar disruptions, the Impact Analyst traces

35
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:43,092
the damage, the Rerouter finds routes, Finance prices each

36
00:01:43,092 --> 00:01:45,860
rescue, and the Verifier rechecks the numbers.

37
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:49,607
Each agent writes its decision as a node, and the next one reads

38
00:01:49,607 --> 00:01:52,819
it from there. The graph is their shared workspace and their

39
00:01:52,819 --> 00:01:53,462
audit trail.

40
00:01:54,812 --> 00:01:59,625
In about a second, they propose rerouting three shipments by sea

41
00:01:59,625 --> 00:02:01,730
for $19,000, saving $68,000.

42
00:02:02,050 --> 00:02:05,026
Spending needs approval, and never from the person who asked.

43
00:02:07,176 --> 00:02:09,971
The Agents view draws the run as the graph they wrote.

44
00:02:09,971 --> 00:02:12,094
On the globe, you can watch them walk it.

45
00:02:15,144 --> 00:02:18,591
Now, memory. Every approved plan records what it expected to

46
00:02:18,591 --> 00:02:20,889
save, and later, what it actually saved.

47
00:02:21,209 --> 00:02:25,879
At Suez in July, air freight cost $96,000 and delivered only 28

48
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:28,399
percent of the saving it promised.

49
00:02:28,399 --> 00:02:30,326
Sea reroutes delivered 95.

50
00:02:31,376 --> 00:02:35,051
In November, LA and Long Beach go on strike:

51
00:02:35,051 --> 00:02:36,889
$681,000 in late fees.

52
00:02:37,209 --> 00:02:41,329
At face value, air-freighting five shipments for $409,000 looks

53
00:02:41,329 --> 00:02:42,310
like a bargain.

54
00:02:42,630 --> 00:02:46,481
But Finance remembers Suez. At 28 percent, the rescue saves less

55
00:02:46,481 --> 00:02:49,971
than it costs, so it recommends no rescue, and shows which

56
00:02:49,971 --> 00:02:51,415
memory changed its mind.

57
00:02:52,865 --> 00:02:57,156
Rerun without memory, and the agents would have paid for the air

58
00:02:57,156 --> 00:03:00,509
freight. Memory avoided an expected $220,000 loss.

59
00:03:03,159 --> 00:03:07,242
Everything runs on FalkorDB. Postgres is only a baseline that

60
00:03:07,242 --> 00:03:08,781
returns identical rows.

61
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:12,695
On deep chains FalkorDB pulls ahead, and we documented three of

62
00:03:12,695 --> 00:03:14,007
its bugs along the way.

63
00:03:14,327 --> 00:03:16,495
Take FalkorDB out, and nothing runs.

64
00:03:18,045 --> 00:03:21,266
Promise Graph. See every broken promise, rescue what matters,

65
00:03:21,266 --> 00:03:23,167
and learn from what actually worked.

